    thinking: "**Analyzing the Request Details**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the user\'s intent. They want me to mimic the design of uploaded images, adapting them for specific needs. The core is design replication with customization in the context of the app. This is key: it\'s not just about a simple style transfer, but adapting the designs, which makes it a more complex, challenging, but interesting task.\n\n\n**Examining the Visual Language**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual language within the first image, specifically the Hiring Timeline Tracker. It showcases a warm, natural aesthetic with a clear structure. I\'m noting the use of a serene botanical palette and its impact. I\'m focusing on the structured left-rail navigation or top-to-bottom card design which seems critical to the user\'s intent to apply a similar layout. The KPI strips, bar breakdowns, and donut chart placement are also vital observations as I start.\n\n\n**Interpreting the New Details**\n\nI\'m now integrating the new information on the \"Beranda\" and Nexus Creative Studio design elements. I\'m noting the tabular overview, the 3-column bottom row with its vertical bar chart, and the editorial pull-quote card\'s botanical accents. The clean mobile typography and section numbering from the Nexus design are also critical; I need to understand how these can inform the design replication process. The user\'s goal seems clear.\n\n\n**Dissecting Visual Composition**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the mobile design elements of the TRODEO Editorial Website image. I am taking note of the full-width dark editorial band, contrasting it with the light text highlights and clean footer lockup. I\'m prioritizing the airy, full-bleed editorial rhythm, particularly the hero section with its kicker accent and bold headline. The integrated quick action/search bar is a core focus - it\'s a visual element I must get right. The mosaic editorial grid layout is also important.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Design Elements**\n\nI\'m now integrating the visual cues from the \"Tiga Langkah Utama Ruang Obat\" and \"Alur DAGUSIBU 4 Langkah\" examples. I\'m focusing on the editorial storytelling and how the pathway diagram works. The dotted path and circular CTA buttons are really interesting for me, as is the use of the 3-card row. I\'m noting the warm color palette and its potential to establish a more approachable layout, along with the banner call-to-action. I need to figure out how to best adapt these elements.\n\n\n**Combining Design Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on synthesizing the final set of design cues. The new *Mountain Meadow* elements \342\200\224 the header band with instant search and category shortcuts \342\200\224 are particularly compelling. I\'m noting the clean card list rows for upcoming events and the interactive checklist card, and I\'m really seeing how all the pieces fit together. This is evolving into a more polished, *Ruang Obat* editorial design, without changing the forbidden files, which is interesting.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Implementation Strategy**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the best implementation plan. The user wants a cohesive visual transformation across the website, incorporating design elements from all four screenshots to meet the Ruang Obat pharmacy education needs. My focus is on decisive action, building the upgrade immediately while preserving existing features, rules, structure, and typography. The goal is to act decisively, in response to their explicit request to \"follow its design\".\n\n\n**Formulating Implementation Plan**\n\nI\'m now integrating the final pieces, focusing on the *Ruang Obat* design system. I\'m focusing on the colors, the typography, and the overall design. I\'ve noted the *Mountain Meadow* elements and their impact and the details that I need to include in the overall design. Now I\'m focusing on the implementation plan and how to best organize all the elements into a single system. I see the final product as a refined *Ruang Obat* editorial design.\n\n\n**Constructing Visual Layout**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the hero section for the main page, inspired by elements from several images. I\'m prioritizing a two-column layout: a left column with a prominent headline, subtitle, and an integrated search bar with category tiles, mirroring features from both the Mountain Meadow and TRODEO Editorial Website designs. The right column will feature a botanical illustration to link to image 1. I\'m focusing on the apothecary shelf illustration with a subtle botanical watermark.\n\n\n**Developing Key Feature Sets**\n\nI\'m now integrating critical features. I\'m focusing on a 4-column KPI strip for `Obat Tercatat` and similar key metrics with clickable buttons and soft botanical styling, inspired by Image 1. I\'m building an analytics and schedule split with a chart, a watchlist card for expiring medicines from Image 1 and Image 4. This is for quick access, and I\'m developing a 3-column insight row.\n\n\n**Refining Actionable Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating the 3-column grid components. I\'m focusing on the *Distribusi Sediaan* spotlight card or similar, along with an editorial quote card with botanical accents. A quick actions menu is also planned. I\'m integrating the numbered section headers, inspired by Nexus and TRODEO, for an editorial showcase. I am also working on the DAGUSIBU pathway from Image 3.\n\n\n**Integrating Editorial Sections**\n\nI\'m now integrating editorial numbering from Image 2 with the serif title and right-aligned subtitle from Image 3. I\'m focusing on a 3-card row inspired by Image 3 and its \"perspectives\". I\'m planning an interactive home safety checklist and styling Image 3\'s dotted pathway for a DAGUSIBU flow. Clicking on the pathway opens the relevant guides or labels.\n\n\n**Finalizing Integration Elements**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the design. I\'m focusing on the editorial banner from Image 3, and I\'m ensuring a smooth transition to the footer with the Nexus typographic hierarchy. All rules are being carefully double-checked: `TentangModal.tsx` remains untouched, fonts are preserved, and specific feature flags remain unchanged. I\'m rigorously excluding changes to restricted files and design elements.\n\n\n**Implementing Interactive Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on interactive elements, ensuring each has a functional handler. I\'m prioritizing safe touch targets with `min-h-[44px]` for mobile and implementing `onNavigate`, `localStorage`, and other event handlers. The design uses `text-brand`, `text-forest`, `text-ink`, and `text-ink-muted` text classes only, while adhering to a `375px` mobile-first safe design. I\'m verifying that all elements connect.\n\n\n**Refining Integration Details**\n\nI\'m now integrating new functionality: by adding an optional prop to a component, the quick actions and pathway become interactive. Specifically, searches in the hero quick search bar open the relevant modal. I\'m focusing on \"BUD\" and \"Parasetamol\", ensuring the search term is correctly passed through. I am simplifying the design so that it is genuinely passing the search term.\n\n\n"
